From a29b93d9cedfb9b20c0158c69d94b527b55918f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Levine <blahm1d@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 12:00:01 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] core: P0012 - wire the display-interrupt source (video
 block -> INTPEND.DI)

The core had full interrupt-taking machinery + a tms34010_video timing
block that emits dpyint_pulse, but nothing instantiated the video block
or connected its pulse to INTPEND, so INTPEND.DI was never set. Smash
T.V.'s main loop is frame-synced on a RAM flag (@0x1086760) that the
display-interrupt handler sets each frame; with no DI firing the loop
spun forever (the post-P0011 steady-loop state).

Measured (tb_yunit_intstate): at the main loop ST.IE=1, INTENB=0x0404
(DINT+INT2), DPYINT=0x9a, VTOTAL=0x120, HTOTAL=0xfc -- the game fully
programmed the display interrupt and only waits on INTPEND.DI.

Fix (tms34010_io_regs.sv): instantiate tms34010_video fed by the stored
timing regs; set INTPEND[INT_DI_BIT] on dpyint_pulse (gated on VTOTAL!=0
to avoid reset-state spam). After this the CPU takes the display
interrupt, the handler sets the flag, and the main loop advances and
calls the per-frame render (ffeaefc0) -- 18 frames rendered. MM unit TBs
still PASS.

New frontier exposed: after ~40 interrupts the first full-context
interrupt handler (epilogue ffe0ef80: MMFM A0-A14; MMFM B0-B5; RETI)
RETIs to 0x00020010 (VRAM garbage). SP doesn't drift at the main loop,
so it's a bad interrupt return -- first real exercise of the core's
push/RETI path. Documented in PATCHES.md P0012.

